title: Game systems and Late Update
intro: Scripts can now run something every frame for a whole kind of thing at once, in the part of the frame they choose, and act once everything has moved.
project: Platformer | Windy Peaks

chapter: A system of your own | Wind for the whole level
say: Until now, a script ran for the one entity it was on. A game system runs once a frame for the whole game, so it can work on every thing of a kind at once.
write: Scripts/Wind.cs | game-systems/Wind.cs
code: Assets/Scripts/Wind.cs | Wind.cs
say: Wind is a system. Its phase says when in the frame it runs: Simulate, after the scripts and before physics. Each frame it pushes every body in the level along.
say: Windy Level is an ordinary script. It adds the wind when it starts, so the wind lasts as long as its entity does. Adding a second Wind would replace the first, so a level that restarts still blows just once.
hidecode
create: Text
rename: Wind Gauge
add: WindyLevel
explain: component:WindyLevel | The script | The script that adds the wind, with its strength.
set: WindyLevel.Strength = 40
play
wait: 2.5
explain: game | Blown along | The player and the slimes drift right without anyone touching the keys: one system, every body.
hold: Left 1.5
say: Walking into it is hard work.
stop

chapter: Late Update | After everything has moved
say: The gauge over the player's head is placed in Late Update. That runs after physics has moved the player and the camera has followed, so the gauge sits right on the player in the same frame, never a step behind.
play
hold: Right 1.5
tap: Space
wait: 1
explain: game | Right on the player | Wherever the wind and the jumps take the player, the gauge keeps up.
stop
say: Every step of Griff's frame now runs in one of eight named phases, in an order that a test pins down, and your systems run after Griff's own in their phase. While a game plays, an agent asking for the editor state sees which systems scripts have added.
